.TH CCOL_DYNAMIC_QUEUE_CREATE 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_dynamic_queue_create \- create a dynamic queue with no size limit
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_dynamic_queue_create(err_str);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a queue with no size limit, kept in a linked list. A send
never blocks, and the queue can grow without limit. Memory comes from
the standard malloc and free.

It is the same as calling ccol_dynamic_queue_create_mp() with NULL
memory management procedures.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
err_str  Optional pointer that gets an error string on failure
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A pointer to the new dynamic queue, or NULL on failure.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
All operations on this queue are thread-safe. When you are done with
the queue, destroy it with ccol_dynamic_queue_destroy().

fork(2) and this queue can be combined in two supported ways: call
fork(2) before you create the queue, or create it and then call fork(2)
followed at once by exec(3). A program that keeps running with no
exec(3) after the fork while a queue from before the fork is live is
not supported. As a defense in depth beyond that rule, the child never
inherits a lock that another thread held, in the locked state, at the
instant of the fork. The queue owns no thread of its own either, so a
queue that a child inherits this way stays fully usable.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_dynamic_queue_create_mp (3),
.BR ccol_dynamic_queue_destroy (3)
